---
import BaseLayout from '~/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { SITE } from '~/consts';
---
<BaseLayout title="About" description="Career-level summary of focus areas across data platforms, streaming, lakehouse, and reliability.">
  <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold sm:text-4xl">About</h1>
  <p class="mt-4 text-lg text-[var(--color-muted)]">
    A short version of who I am and what I tend to write about. Not a résumé.
  </p>

  <section class="prose prose-engineering mt-8">
    <p>
      I've spent the last <strong>16+ years</strong> building data systems,
      mostly in places where things were either growing fast enough or breaking
      often enough to make the platform interesting. The job titles changed,
      the stack changed several times, but the work didn't really. I design
      and run systems that move a lot of data, expose it for analytics and
      products downstream, and try to keep the result something a small team
      can actually operate without burning out.
    </p>

    <p>
      The tooling has gone through a few generations on me. On-prem Hadoop,
      then MPP warehouses, then big monolithic ETL jobs, and now object-storage
      lakehouses with open table formats and decoupled compute. The questions
      underneath are stubbornly the same ones. How do pipelines stay correct
      when something upstream fails halfway through? Where should state
      actually live? And what should the platform refuse to do, even when it
      could?
    </p>

    <h2>What I work on</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Distributed data systems.</strong> Partitioning, consistency, fault tolerance, and the operational tax that comes with each design choice.</li>
      <li><strong>Streaming architecture.</strong> Event time vs processing time, exactly-once (when it actually means anything), state backends, and the line between streaming and serving.</li>
      <li><strong>Data platforms and lakehouse architecture.</strong> Open table formats, catalog design, multi-engine analytics, and the constant tug-of-war between flexibility and standardization.</li>
      <li><strong>Cloud data engineering on AWS, GCP, and Azure.</strong> Managed warehouses, object storage as the system of record, and the FinOps reality nobody quite signs up for.</li>
      <li><strong>Reliability and observability.</strong> Freshness, lineage, contracts, and treating data products with the same operational seriousness as services.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>How I think about the work</h2>
    <p>
      Data systems are sociotechnical. The hard problems are rarely algorithmic.
      They're usually about ownership boundaries, who's allowed to change what,
      and what happens when something upstream shifts quietly. I try to write
      about the parts that travel well across stacks: patterns, tradeoffs, and
      where the ecosystem seems to be heading. Tutorials for a specific vendor
      go stale fast, so I mostly leave those to the docs.
    </p>

    <h2>About this site</h2>
    <p>
      This is a working notebook more than a blog. Posts are aimed at
      senior-engineer-level architecture and systems thinking, not step-by-step
      walkthroughs. Source is on <a href={SITE.github} target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub</a>;
      everything is statically generated with Astro and MDX.
    </p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
